        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement China Politics Why Trump’s visit to Beijing has Taiwan on edge about US arms sales Taipei fears US leader’s transactional approach to diplomacy could turn the island into a bargaining chip for a broader Sino-American deal 3 -MIN READ 3 -MIN 1 Listen Lawrence Chung in Taipei Published: 10:30pm, 12 May 2026 Updated: 10:35pm, 12 May 2026 Taiwan is watching the coming summit between US President Donald Trump and Chinese leader with unease, concerned that any discussions over arms sales could see the island used as leverage in a broader Sino-American deal. Trump arrives in Beijing on Wednesday for a three-day state visit. According to the White House , the engagements will include a bilateral meeting with Xi and a state banquet on Thursday, as well as a working lunch before Trump departs on Friday. Speaking at the White House on Monday, Trump said arms sales to Taiwan would be among the issues discussed with Xi, who has long opposed Washington’s military support for the island. Advertisement The remarks immediately reignited debate in Taiwan over whether Trump’s transactional approach to diplomacy could undermine the “Six Assurances” – long-standing US commitments that Washington would not consult Beijing in advance on arms sales to Taiwan. Taipei moved quickly to reassure the public. Advertisement On Tuesday, Taiwanese foreign ministry spokesman Hsiao Kuang-wei said the island was “closely monitoring US-China interactions and maintaining close communication with Washington” to safeguard its interests. 08:56 Why Xi Jinping is hosting world leaders in China instead of travelling abroad to see them Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
